. Maine's trust laws are generally standard UTC and not particularly favorable for aggressive asset protection or dynasty planning compared to DAPT/abolished-perpetuities states.","unique_factors":"Maine was one of the earlier UTC adopters (2003 legislation, 2005 effective). Maine's UTC is codified as Title 18-B, a standalone title. Maine has specific provisions for pet trusts (18-B M.R.S. §408) following the UTC model. Maine's perpetuities law follows the Uniform Statutory Rule Against Perpetuities with a 90-year wait-and-see period, which is more permissive than the common law rule but less favorable than states that have abolished the rule entirely. Maine does not have a state estate or inheritance tax. Maine's trust code includes specific provisions for decanting (18-B M.R.S. §414B) and trust protectors. Maine allows directed trustee arrangements.","provenance":{"sources":[{"label":"Me. Rev. Stat. tit. 18-B, § 1013","url":"https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/18-B/title18-Bsec1013.html","kind":"official"}],"verified_as_of":"2026-09-17","human_legal_reviewed":false,"evidence":"curl -> HTTP 200; page text '§1013: Certification of trust' within Title 18-B, Ch. 10 navigator; statute number '§1013' present."}};
